 "cushionNotice": "No single row is the story. Stack them: a campo family meets a broken leg, a flood or a death with no contract, no paid leave, no crop or life insurance, maybe a bank account, no title to borrow against, and credit at 36–58% a year from a bank or about 20% a month from a *prestamista*. What catches them is family — a remittance, a neighbor, the *san*, the colmado's credit, the cooperador's list. Most RJ families meet the same events with five or six cushions they did not build and rarely see. That insulation is the fence we can't see from inside (2b).",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Paid leave, insurance, a bank that lends at single digits, water that is clean every day without anyone asking who dug for it. See 2c."
  },
  {
   "h": "The resort Dominican Republic",
   "text": "Most Americans who know the DR know Punta Cana: an all-inclusive behind a wall, a beach, a wristband. Some students' families have been. That picture puts the country on the far side of a gate — Dominicans as staff, the countryside as the view from the airport bus. The campo is a different country from the resort, and the resort is built to keep it out of sight."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical",
   "text": "A week in one campo, among families who welcome guests. The aqueduct, the paint and the proyectico can make the trip feel like our project — but the trench was started before we came and the committee runs it after we leave."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "We arrive not knowing the occupation, Trujillo, 1965 or why so many families have someone in New York. Baseball and merengue stand in for a country. Fast Cibao Spanish makes people seem harder to follow than they are. Haiti arrives as a set of opinions students have absorbed, not knowledge. And place names blur: region, town and campo are mixed up."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "Pity — needing people to be poor so the trip means something; or they have so little but they are so happy which erases the structure. The warmth of host families can be read as simple, when hospitality here is a practiced skill and a moral commitment. Working hard on the aqueduct can make us feel we have paid our way."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We brought them water — the savior story. Both put us at the center; the better question is whose work it is. We're so blessed — reading our comfort as God's favor. Treating devotion to the Virgen de la Altagracia or the Santo Cerro as folklore instead of living Catholic faith."
  }
 ],
